<!--
  Checked-in UiAutomator2 /source fixture: Chrome's Touch-To-Fill "Use saved
  password?" credential sheet, captured while it OBSCURED an Auth0 login form
  inside a Chrome Custom Tab. The companion to auth0_android_source.xml, which
  is the same page with no sheet up.

  Why this tree matters: while the sheet is showing, the entire web-content
  subtree is ABSENT from the accessibility tree — an xpath for the form's own
  inputs (e.g. //*[@resource-id="email"]) matches nothing here. The sheet both
  blocks ACTION_SET_TEXT on the field and invalidates any element handle
  resolved before it appeared, so detection must key off the sheet itself.

  Stable detection keys (resource-id, locale-independent):
    com.android.chrome:id/bottom_sheet
    com.android.chrome:id/bottom_sheet_content
    com.android.chrome:id/sheet_item_list
    com.android.chrome:id/touch_to_fill_sheet_title

  Do NOT key detection off any human-readable string in this tree. The title
  text ("Use saved password?"), the content-desc on bottom_sheet ("List of
  credentials to be filled on touch.") and the per-row "Password for ... saved
  for ..." labels are all localised by Chrome.

  Capture conditions: Pixel 7a, Android 16 (SDK 36), Chrome 150.0.7871.187,
  Appium 3.5.2 / uiautomator2 8.2.2. The sheet only appears when the Chrome
  profile has a saved password for the origin being loaded, which is why it
  does not reproduce on a fresh profile.

  IF YOU RE-CAPTURE THIS FILE, SCRUB IT BEFORE COMMITTING. A raw capture is
  PII-bearing by construction: this sheet enumerates the device profile's real
  saved credentials, so the dump will contain actual account usernames and the
  origins they were saved for. In this file every username has been replaced
  with saved.userN@example.com and every origin with login.*.example.com;
  bounds, class, package, clickable and all com.android.chrome:* resource-ids
  are untouched so the tree stays faithful for parser and detection tests.
-->
